What you should be able to explain afterwards
By the end of the activity you should be able to explain:

what information a CIF contains;
the difference between the asymmetric unit and the complete unit cell;
how symmetry operations generate equivalent atomic positions;
what a Wyckoff position and its multiplicity represent;
why a numerical symmetry search requires a tolerance;
why visualizing a structure is useful for inspection but does not replace a crystallographic analysis.

Your answers are written directly in the notebooks. No separate report is required.

Laboratory path
The practical work will progressively connect crystal structures, energetic models, physical properties and mineral thermodynamics:

crystal structures, CIF files and symmetry;
first-principles energy and geometry optimization;
energy-volume data and equations of state;
vibrations, phonons and thermodynamic properties.

Short scientific questions are answered directly in the laboratory notebooks. The notebooks are part of your personal workspace and should be saved and synchronized regularly.

Stop the Codespace






ImportantClosing the browser tab does not stop the Codespace



Always stop the Codespace explicitly when you finish working.


The most reliable method is:

open https://github.com/codespaces;
locate your Mineral Science Codespace;
select the … menu;
select Stop codespace.

You can also stop it from inside Visual Studio Code:

press Ctrl+Shift+P on Windows/Linux or Cmd+Shift+P on macOS;
search for Codespaces: Stop Current Codespace;
select the command.

Stopping a Codespace ends the running virtual machine while preserving saved files for the next session. It also avoids unnecessary compute usage.
Do not delete your Codespace as part of the normal workflow. Delete it only when explicitly instructed or when you are certain that all required work has already been synchronized to GitHub.

What you need on your own computer
You need:

a modern web browser;
a GitHub account;
an internet connection;
later in the course, an SSH/SFTP client for access to CluMiner.

Everything, from Python environment, Jupyter notebooks, and other tools are provided inside the Codespace.
Continue with Create your personal workspace.
